2. Database Requirements
The platform will have two broad categories of data.
A. Operational Data
Examples:
Users
Government agencies
Emergency responders
Disaster incidents
Field reports
Shelters
Evacuation routes
Alerts
Damage assessments
B. Climate/GIS Data
Examples:
Rainfall
Temperature
Humidity
Wind
Soil moisture
River levels
Historical flood events
Satellite-derived information
Elevation
Land cover
Geographic boundaries

Recommended Database:
Azure Database for PostgreSQL + PostGIS

PostGIS is particularly important because the platform is expected to provide GIS/risk maps, locations, flood zones and spatial analysis.

Example:
PostgreSQL
│
├── users
├── organizations
├── disaster_events
├── field_reports
├── damage_assessments
├── shelters
├── alerts
│
├── climate_observations
├── rainfall
├── temperature
├── soil_moisture
├── river_levels
│
└── spatial_layers
    ├── flood_zones
    ├── roads
    ├── hospitals
    ├── schools
    └── administrative_boundaries

Initial database sizing
For the MVP, start small and design for scaling.
The Cloud Engineer should determine:
.CPU
.RAM
.Storage
.IOPS
.Connection limits
.Backup retention
.Expected database growth
The sizing should be based on the expected number of users, frequency of climate-data ingestion and volume of GIS/satellite data rather than arbitrarily choosing a large production SKU.

3. Storage Requirements
The platform will potentially generate very large datasets, especially from satellite imagery and field photographs.

Therefore, do not store large images and files directly inside PostgreSQL.

Use:
Azure Blob Storage
Suggested structure:
climate-data/
│
├── rainfall/
├── weather/
├── satellite/
├── soil-moisture/
├── elevation/
│
├── disaster-data/
│
├── field-reports/
├── damage-assessment/
├── photographs/
│
└── backups/

Database stores:
Report ID
File URL
File Type
Location
Timestamp
User
Description

Blob Storage stores:
Satellite image
Damage photograph
Field attachment
Report document
Large climate dataset

This keeps the database efficient while allowing the platform to accommodate large datasets.

4. Compute Requirements
The main compute workload will come from:
Application workloads
.Web dashboard
.REST APIs
.Mobile backend
.Authentication
.Disaster reporting
.Alert processing

Data workloads
.Data ingestion
.Data cleaning
.Data transformation
.Risk calculations
.GIS processing
.Analytical workloads
.Flood-risk calculations
.Drought analysis
.Heat-risk analysis
.Predictive modelling

For Phase 1, I recommend:
Azure App Service
        │
        ├── Web Application
        └── REST API
For heavier analytics later:
Azure App Service
        +
Azure Functions / Container Apps
        +
Dedicated data-processing compute
The important principle is not to run expensive analytical processing continuously on the web server.
